import{_ as t,o as a,c as o,a2 as r}from"./chunks/framework.CdvW4CUT.js";const m=JSON.parse('{"title":"Standard-library runtime contracts","description":"","frontmatter":{"head":[["link",{"rel":"canonical","href":"https://cx-pl.io/generated/standard-library-contracts.html"}],["meta",{"property":"og:url","content":"https://cx-pl.io/generated/standard-library-contracts.html"}]]},"headers":[],"relativePath":"generated/standard-library-contracts.md","filePath":"generated/standard-library-contracts.md","lastUpdated":null}'),n={name:"generated/standard-library-contracts.md"};function i(s,e,c,d,l,h){return a(),o("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[r('<blockquote><p>Imported from <a href="https://github.com/cx-pl/cxcore/blob/26b4868617f19db79686292d1b85315b20f8e160/docs/standard-library-contracts.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">cxcore</a> at revision <code>26b4868617f19db79686292d1b85315b20f8e160</code>. This snapshot is updated deliberately; it does not track repository changes automatically.</p></blockquote><h1 id="standard-library-runtime-contracts" tabindex="-1">Standard-library runtime contracts <a class="header-anchor" href="#standard-library-runtime-contracts" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Standard-library runtime contracts&quot;">​</a></h1><p>These contracts describe the current C runtime. CX allocations are managed by a non-moving conservative collector with explicit native worker registration and cooperative stop-the-world coordination and public <code>System.Threading</code> workers, mutexes, and atomic integer cells. The collector design and limits are documented in <a href="https://github.com/cx-pl/cxcore/blob/26b4868617f19db79686292d1b85315b20f8e160/docs/managed-memory-design.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>managed-memory-design.md</code></a>. The ABI and ownership rules remain experimental.</p><h2 id="allocation-and-ownership" tabindex="-1">Allocation and ownership <a class="header-anchor" href="#allocation-and-ownership" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Allocation and ownership&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><code>Memory.Alloc</code> returns zero-initialized, collector-tracked storage. A zero-byte request returns a non-null allocation that can be passed to <code>Memory.Free</code>.</li><li><code>Memory.Realloc(block, 0)</code> frees <code>block</code> and returns null. For a nonzero size, a failed reallocation terminates the process; it never returns a lost old pointer.</li><li><code>Memory.Copy</code> is a no-op for size zero. Nonzero copies require valid, non-overlapping ranges, as with C <code>memcpy</code>.</li><li>Built-in <code>T[]</code> storage and the public <code>System.Collections.Array&amp;lt;T&amp;gt;</code> API use the same <code>System.Collections.Array</code> runtime structure and own a zero-initialized element buffer allocated by <code>cx_array_new</code>. The public type is declared in <code>src/System/Collections/Array.cx</code> and exposes <code>Length</code>, <code>Get</code>, and <code>Set</code>. The current explicit cleanup is <code>Memory.Free(array-&amp;gt;_data)</code> followed by <code>Memory.Free(array)</code>. <code>cx_array_at</code> returns null for a null array, an out-of-range index, invalid zero-sized elements, or missing backing storage.</li><li>Runtime-owned allocations go through <code>Memory.Alloc</code> and are eligible for collection when unreachable. <code>Memory.Free</code> immediately unregisters and releases a block; freeing a block while a live reference may be used is invalid. <code>Memory.Realloc</code> tracks the resized block, but references invalidated by a move must not be used.</li><li><code>Nullable</code> is a one-pointer value. Its constructor and <code>Value</code> setter store a non-owning pointer; compiler-generated lifts call <code>Nullable&amp;lt;T&amp;gt;.CreateValueStorage</code>, which allocates a copy of the supplied bytes through <code>Memory.Alloc</code>; the payload is traced conservatively with the rest of the managed heap.</li><li><code>Object</code> initializes its runtime vtable. Ordinary objects leave <code>_box</code> null; boxed value objects use it to retain their managed box header and payload. No finalizers run. The non-moving collector may retain unreachable blocks when stale pointer-shaped values remain on a scanned stack or in a registered root.</li><li><code>DateTime</code> component construction validates the Gregorian date and time fields. <code>AddMonths</code> uses calendar months, clamps the day to the last day of the destination month, and keeps the time-of-day and offset. Invalid components and results outside the stored millisecond range write a diagnostic and exit with failure.</li></ul><h2 id="strings-characters-and-console" tabindex="-1">Strings, characters, and console <a class="header-anchor" href="#strings-characters-and-console" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Strings, characters, and console&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>String data is UTF-8 and <code>_length</code> is a byte count. <code>String.Length</code> counts Unicode scalar values, and <code>String.Item</code> indexes those scalar values. The item getter returns <code>-1</code> for an invalid index or malformed UTF-8 sequence. <code>Length</code> counts each malformed byte as one replacement position so it stays bounded by the stored byte length.</li><li><code>String ==</code>, <code>String !=</code>, <code>Equals</code>, and <code>GetHashCode</code> use the stored UTF-8 bytes; equal byte sequences compare equal and produce the same hash. <code>ToString()</code> returns the same string instance. String constructors initialize the <code>String</code> vtable after the base <code>Object</code> constructor initializes the base object state.</li><li><code>String &amp;lt;</code>, <code>&amp;lt;=</code>, <code>&amp;gt;</code>, and <code>&amp;gt;=</code> compare UTF-8 byte sequences lexicographically. For valid UTF-8, this gives Unicode scalar value ordering. A null string sorts before a non-null string when the comparison is made through typed string values.</li><li><code>String + String</code> copies both UTF-8 byte sequences into a new managed string. A null operand contributes no bytes. Length overflow and allocation failure terminate according to the runtime allocation contract.</li><li><code>String.__constructor(length, data)</code> borrows an immutable byte buffer. The buffer must remain alive and unchanged while the string uses it. String literals borrow static storage. The character-fill constructor allocates its encoded UTF-8 buffer; callers may release it with <code>Memory.Free</code> only when no live reference can use it.</li><li><code>Char</code> is a Unicode scalar value in a 32-bit integer. <code>Char.NumBytes</code> returns its UTF-8 encoded width, or <code>-1</code> for a surrogate or value above U+10FFFF. Current <code>IsDigit</code>, <code>IsLetter</code>, case conversion, and whitespace helpers are ASCII-only.</li><li><code>Console.Read(length)</code> reads at most <code>length</code> bytes. <code>ReadLine()</code> reads through a newline or EOF and grows its buffer as needed. Both return a heap-allocated String object with heap-allocated contents; those allocations are traced. Callers may release them with <code>Memory.Free</code> when no live reference can use them. <code>Console.Write</code> writes the stored UTF-8 bytes unchanged and returns the number of bytes written.</li></ul><p>Allocation failures in <code>Memory.Alloc</code> and nonzero <code>Memory.Realloc</code> write <code>Out of memory</code> to standard error and exit with failure. The old allocation remains untouched when reallocation fails before process termination. Bounds, ownership, and UTF-8 errors do not currently raise typed CX exceptions.</p><h2 id="shared-library-declarations" tabindex="-1">Shared-library declarations <a class="header-anchor" href="#shared-library-declarations" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Shared-library declarations&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>CX_API</code> marks runtime functions as exports while building <code>cxcore</code>, imports for dynamic consumers, and undecorated declarations for static consumers. Public runtime data uses <code>CX_CXCORE_DATA_API</code> with the same producer/consumer/static behavior. CMake propagates <code>CX_STATIC_LINK</code> from static <code>cxcore</code> targets to their consumers.</p><h2 id="managed-memory-limits" tabindex="-1">Managed-memory limits <a class="header-anchor" href="#managed-memory-limits" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Managed-memory limits&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The collector scans registered thread stacks, saved registers, managed blocks, and registered static-field regions. Generated modules register their static fields; native C code must register static storage that retains managed pointers. Heap operations from an unregistered OS thread fail. Native workers must attach, poll safepoints, park during native-only blocking calls, and detach before exit; see <a href="https://github.com/cx-pl/cxcore/blob/26b4868617f19db79686292d1b85315b20f8e160/docs/threading-gc-design.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">threading-gc-design.md</a>. Application fields require their own synchronization. Synchronous native calls on a running attached thread retain their stack roots; references retained beyond the call must be kept in a registered root or another managed block. Foreign callbacks use owned attachment scopes and removable root handles; reentry from a parked native-only blocking callback still requires a future explicit adapter.</p><h2 id="platform-support" tabindex="-1">Platform support <a class="header-anchor" href="#platform-support" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Platform support&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The runtime supports Windows through MSVC and x64 Linux through GCC. Linux selects native environment and system-clock providers and the platform-specific managed-memory helpers. Static and shared Linux targets build and pass the native runtime test suite in the repository&#39;s Docker development container. macOS remains unsupported.</p><h2 id="native-interop-and-text" tabindex="-1">Native interop and text <a class="header-anchor" href="#native-interop-and-text" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Native interop and text&quot;">​</a></h2><p>wchar is a 16-bit UTF-16 code unit on every target. System.WideString owns immutable copied storage; Length/Item count units and ScalarCount/ScalarAt validate scalars. Equality, hashing and ordinal comparison use units. Empty values and embedded zeros are preserved. String.ToWideString and the Object.ToString override on WideString transcode explicitly, without normalization or implicit conversion. Malformed UTF-8 or unpaired UTF-16 throws EncodingException with a byte/unit Position.</p><p>System.Interop.NativeBuffer owns malloc storage. AllocateAligned takes a power-of-two alignment for stronger SDK layouts. FromUtf8/FromUtf16 copy input with optional termination; ByteLength includes that terminator. ToUtf8String/ToWideString copy explicit output lengths without scanning for zero. Dispose is explicit/idempotent; never dispose while native code retains Data. SDK objects and buffers require their own allocator/destructor, not Memory.Alloc or NativeBuffer.Dispose.</p><p>NativeLibrary keeps loaded handles until process exit; Resolve&lt;T&gt; returns typed native functions or throws a load/symbol error. NativeError holds immediate TLS errno/last-error snapshots. NativeCallback.Register&lt;T&gt; roots a closure and optional owner until successful SDK unregistration and completion of in-flight callbacks. Unregister failure permits retry; self-unregistration is rejected. LastException survives close, and late callbacks receive their configured failure result. Small context tombstones stay until process exit; managed payloads are freed on close. Callbacks catch CX exceptions before foreign frames and restore native TLS state. See <a href="https://github.com/cx-pl/cxcore/blob/26b4868617f19db79686292d1b85315b20f8e160/docs/native-interop-design.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">native-interop-design.md</a> for ABI and lifetime rules.</p>',18)])])}const p=t(n,[["render",i]]);export{m as __pageData,p as default};
